# Asynchronous pending presentation

{% section id="condition" title="Why this opens now" %}

Decision 9 (2026-08-07) deferred asynchronous pending presentation out of weft#16
as unrequested scope, with the condition recorded as amendment A4 in
`docs/brand-package/12-input-heuristics.md`: *when asynchronous pending
presentation is introduced — commit starts evaluation; the consumer may prevent
progression while pending; Weft only presents the supplied pending or result
state.* Katie has requested the stage, and the consumer need is demonstrated:
Heddle's `SourceValidationStatus` is a live seven-state asynchronous vocabulary
that a pending/success/error model cannot express.

The A4 clause deliberately does not say Weft blocks navigation or submission —
it does not and will not. Decisions 7 and 10 stand: Weft never evaluates, and
the spy-based ownership guard in `commit-boundary.test.tsx` must keep passing
untouched.

{% /section %}

{% section id="groundtruth" title="Ground truth: the consumer's vocabulary, measured" %}

From `heddle/src/app/services/sourceValidationService.ts` and its consumers,
read 2026-08-13 (extending the four findings the handoff carried):

- **Seven statuses**: `validating | reachable | unreachable | degraded |
  blocked | unsupported-in-browser | unknown`.
- **The service itself never returns `validating` or `unknown`.** Pending is
  consumer-side: `SessionContextPanel` renders `Status: validating` when the
  result record is *absent* (line 685) — pending is the absence of an answer,
  not a status the check reports. `unknown` is likewise the consumer's
  not-yet-asked value. The remaining five are settled results.
- **The consumer already owns a tone mapping.** `severityForStatus` maps status
  → `ok | info | warning | danger`: reachable→ok; blocked, unreachable→danger;
  degraded, unsupported-in-browser→warning; validating, unknown→info.
  `degraded` is a *warning-toned result that is neither success nor failure* —
  `readable` stays true under it. This is the status-not-boolean finding as
  shipped code.
- **Results carry consumer text**: a `guidance` sentence and classified
  `warnings[]`. The words are the consumer's; no fixed vocabulary could hold
  them.
- **Weft precedent, distinct surface**: `ProviderStatusBadge` (in Weft since the
  Session Context work) presents a five-value *provider* vocabulary as a capsule
  badge. It is not field-attached and carries no describedby relationship; it is
  evidence that consumers keep inventing status presentation when the field
  doesn't offer one, not a base to extend.
- `SessionContextPanel`'s current rendering is the defect this stage exists to
  replace: a bare string, colour from severity as the only visual signal (the
  1.4.1 class the owner-validation round just purged from Weft), no
  describedby relationship, no reduced-motion consideration.

**Consequence for the contract**: Weft cannot own Heddle's seven statuses — the
next consumer will have seven different ones. What Weft standardises is the
*presentation contract*: a consumer-supplied pending flag or result tone plus
consumer-supplied text, rendered in the hint slot with the describedby, motion,
and exposure rules handled once. The consumer keeps the mapping from its own
vocabulary onto that contract, exactly as it keeps validation itself
(decision 7).

{% /section %}

{% section id="contract" title="The presentation contract" %}

**Both layers, parity, no new allowlist entry** — this is CSS + markup
convention in the plain layer and a thin component in React; no JavaScript
behaviour is required for the plain layer to reach the same rendering.

- **Placement: the hint slot.** The field's right edge is taken (chevron,
  search clear, error glyph — asserted by `error-icon.spec.ts`); the pending
  affordance adds nothing there. Plain CSS: `.weft-field-hint.is-pending`.
  React: a new `FormStatus` member of the `Form` family rendering into the same
  slot. `Input` gains no prop (counter-guard: props snapshot untouched for
  `input`).
- **Pending is text with a pulsing dot**, reusing `weft-pulse`, whose final
  keyframe is opacity 1 — under Weft's reduced-motion freeze (0.001ms, one
  iteration) the dot measures static-visible rather than reading as a hung
  field. The dot is also the non-colour shape signal for pending.
- **Results are a tone axis, not a boolean**: `ok | info | warn | stop`,
  landing on the existing tokens `--weft-ok / --weft-info / --weft-warn /
  --weft-stop` (the same four axes as Heddle's `severityForStatus`; `warn`
  carries `degraded`). The consumer's text carries the meaning; tone colour
  reinforces and is never the only signal distinguishing two states the
  consumer renders with identical text — specimens must not ship identical-text
  tone pairs. A `stop`-toned status is presentation only: whether the field is
  *invalid* (`aria-invalid`, error machinery, A5 message) stays the consumer's
  call, per decision 7.
- **Describedby: A5's order extended, not reordered.** One ordered reference
  list: error id first, status id second, help id third. Error keeps first
  position (urgent thing first — A5's rationale); status precedes durable help
  because it is the newest fact about the field. Existing pairs keep their
  relative order, so every shipped A5 case is unchanged. Plain-CSS convention:
  status ids end `-status`, enforced by the specimen structural gates alongside
  the existing `-hint`/`-error` rules.
- **`aria-busy` on the control while pending**, per the P2-era measurement
  (`busy: 1` with the description resolving). React: `FormStatus`'s pending
  registration reaches the control through the existing item context. Plain
  CSS: a markup convention — a control whose describedby references an
  `is-pending` hint carries `aria-busy="true"` — enforced page-wide by the
  specimen gate in both directions (pending hint without busy fails; busy
  without a pending hint fails).
- **Replacement, not stacking.** A status change replaces the text under the
  same id; ids never duplicate in the reference list. Staleness and
  cancellation are consumer-owned — the consumer decides *what* state to
  supply and when; Weft's whole behaviour is to present the current supplied
  state.
- **Exposure, never announcement.** Every claim in tests and prose: the
  description *resolves* in the accessibility tree. A change to a describedby
  target is not a dependable live update, and nothing here claims otherwise.

**Non-goals**: evaluation of validity (decisions 7/10); blocking navigation,
submission, or progression (A4's own clause); announcement claims; QuietField /
DisclosureField (decision 8 — still waiting on two independent consumers);
Heddle's own migration of `SessionContextPanel` (its repo, after release).

{% /section %}

{% section id="tests" title="Test manifest (written before implementation)" %}

| File | Claims |
|---|---|
| `src/ui/__tests__/form-status.test.tsx` | FormStatus renders supplied text in the hint slot; pending dot present; tone attribute for each of the four tones; describedby order error→status→help asserted by POSITION across the A5 case matrix extended with status (help+status; error+status; all three; error removed, status+help survive; status removed, ids leave the list; no duplicated id); replacement under a stable id; `aria-busy` on the control while pending and gone when not; ownership spies — supplying pending/status calls no consumer callback, writes no value, touches no react-hook-form state. |
| `src/ui/__tests__/source-validation-vocabulary.test.tsx` | The consumer-shaped transition suite against Heddle's seven states: a mapping fixture (status → {pending | tone, text}) drives FormStatus through validating→reachable, validating→degraded (warning result, field NOT invalid, readable), validating→unreachable (consumer chooses error machinery; status yields to A5 message order), reachable→validating (revalidation), unknown and unsupported-in-browser; each transition asserts the exposed describedby state and that Weft performed no evaluation (spies). |
| `tests/contract/input-pending.spec.ts` | Rendered: the pending dot measures opacity 1 (static-visible) under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` and animates otherwise; the field's right edge is unchanged by pending (no new painted element in the reserve; padding untouched); tone colours resolve from the four tokens per palette × theme; specimen describedby order holds error→status→hint. |
| `scripts/__tests__/input-specimens.node.mjs` (extended) | Structural gates: `-status` id convention resolves; order error→status→hint page-wide; `aria-busy` ⇄ `is-pending` pairing both directions; no identical-text tone-pair specimen. |
| Counter-guards | `input` props snapshot untouched; `commit-boundary.test.tsx` ownership spies untouched and passing; `css/weft.css` diff additive-only (`check-pure-token-file`); no new parity gap (both layers ship). |

Every guard added here gets probed by reintroducing its violation before it is
believed. A guard that has not failed has not been verified.

{% /section %}

